1
00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:05.472
Welcome to Document AI: From OCR to Agentic Doc Extraction,

2
00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:08.475
built in partnership with LandingAI where I'm executive chairman.

3
00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.176
A lot of data is locked

4
00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.778
up in PDF files and other documents

5
00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:15.181
on our laptops, on the web, and in company's cloud storage.

6
00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:18.785
In this course, you'll implement document processing pipelines

7
00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.787
that transform your complex documents

8
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.391
into LLM-ready markdown text and extracts information.

9
00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:26.493
for analysis.

10
00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:30.697
You begin by explaining traditional OCR which only extracts text

11
00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:33.900
and then add techniques to detect document structure

12
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.902
and identify visual components

13
00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:39.606
such as tables with complex formatting like merged cells

14
00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:43.710
or charts with captions or forms with checkboxes.

15
00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.281
You'll implement an agentic workflow that combines layout detection

16
00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.083
with LLM-based reasoning.

17
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.286
Then, you'll learn to use Agentic Document Extraction,

18
00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.189
or ADE, a tool designed by LandingAI

19
00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:59.092
that automates this entire workflow for you.

20
00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.295
I'm delighted that the instructors for this course are David Park,

21
00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.298
who's Senior Director of Applied AI, and Andrea Kropp,

22
00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.100
who is an Applied AI engineer.

23
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.803
Both have helped many developers build complex

24
00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:11.504
document AI systems.

25
00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:15.475
Thanks Andrew. We're excited to teach this course together.

26
00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:20.180
Traditional OCR systems worked by segmenting documents into individual characters

27
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.383
and then classifying each character using supervised learning.

28
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.186
These systems could extract characters accurately,

29
00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:28.254
but they lacked understanding of how

30
00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:30.290
different parts of a document hang together,

31
00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.194
such as the document structure or tables or forms,

32
00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.262
or even what is the reading

33
00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.698
order of different elements of a document.

34
00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:41.901
Landing AI's ADE takes a different approach.

35
00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:45.505
It treats each page of a document as an image

36
00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:47.807
and breaks that image down into parts

37
00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:52.112
and then extracts from each part separately using an agentic workflow.

38
00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:55.315
ADE treats documents as visual objects in which

39
00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:59.519
meaning is encoded in layout structure and spatial relationships.

40
00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:03.123
It uses custom visual models that directly interpret

41
00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:07.527
complex tables, graphs, images, charts, and other elements.

42
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.029
and grounds every extracted piece of text

43
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.132
to a precise location on the page.

44
00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.435
On top of this, ADE uses an agentic orchestration layer

45
00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.737
that decomposes complex documents

46
00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.540
into smaller sections for careful examination

47
00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.343
in several steps.

48
00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:27.080
Humans don't process a document with a single glance.

49
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.483
Instead, we examine the different parts of the document

50
00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:34.787
to pull out information piece by piece in multiple iterations.

51
00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:39.492
ADE works the same way. For example, given a complex document, ADE

52
00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:43.730
it might extract a table and then further extract the table structure,

53
00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.634
identifying rows, columns, merged cells, and so on.

54
00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:51.538
In this course, you'll explore traditional OCR-based processing pipelines

55
00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:55.341
to see what they can do and also their limitations.

56
00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:59.112
You'll also build your own agentic document workflow from scratch.

57
00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:03.416
Then you'll learn how to use ADE to process complex documents

58
00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:06.619
and build a pipeline to parse mixed documents

59
00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.821
and extract required key-value pairs.

60
00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:13.159
You'll also learn to integrate ADE extracted information

61
00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.161
into RAG applications.

62
00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:17.864
And then implement a cloud-based version on AWS

63
00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.166
using an event-driven architecture

64
00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:23.870
that automatically triggers ADE for document processing

65
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.872
whenever new documents appear.

66
00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.741
Many people have worked to create this course.

67
00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.044
I'd like to thank from LandingAI,

68
00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:33.446
Ava Xia, and from DeepLearning.AI,

69
00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:36.249
Hawraa Salami and Christopher Policastro.

70
00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:40.453
In the first lesson, you start by looking at traditional OCR models.

71
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.022
Let's go to the next video and dive in.
